WEBVTT

NOTE
Journey: Advocates — Week 8 Leader Video
Unit 2, Lesson 4: Naturalism

1
00:00:05.810 --> 00:00:11.810
So we've talked about world views, but what world views should we talk about?

2
00:00:11.810 --> 00:00:15.810
Talk about what world views are, but what world views should we actually talk about?

3
00:00:15.810 --> 00:00:19.810
What world views are your students most exposed to?

4
00:00:19.810 --> 00:00:20.810
Now there's a lot.

5
00:00:20.810 --> 00:00:24.810
We're only gonna touch a couple of the most popular world views

6
00:00:24.810 --> 00:00:26.810
and we're gonna talk about some of the more dominant ones

7
00:00:26.810 --> 00:00:29.810
as well as what a biblical world view starts to look like.

8
00:00:29.810 --> 00:00:34.810
But the first one that we're gonna talk about is the idea of naturalism.

9
00:00:35.810 --> 00:00:41.810
Now you can tell right out of the gate probably the idea of naturalism has the idea of nature.

10
00:00:41.810 --> 00:00:43.810
Think of your senses.

11
00:00:43.810 --> 00:00:47.810
Think of the five senses that you have, what you can taste, touch, smell, and feel.

12
00:00:47.810 --> 00:00:53.810
Naturalism teaches that only those things are what constitutes reality

13
00:00:53.810 --> 00:00:56.810
and there we're back to that word again.

14
00:00:56.810 --> 00:00:58.810
What is reality?

15
00:00:58.810 --> 00:01:03.810
So when we're talking about these other world views, they're doing the same thing that we're trying to do.

16
00:01:03.810 --> 00:01:07.810
Trying to talk about what is reality and what is truth.

17
00:01:07.810 --> 00:01:12.810
How do we interpret these things in light of what naturalism actually is?

18
00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:17.810
So those who believe in naturalism are trying to take the five senses

19
00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:22.810
and interpret reality and dictate what is true based on those factors.

20
00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:24.810
That's essentially what they're doing.

21
00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:28.810
And this really, if you think about your students, especially those in public schools,

22
00:01:28.810 --> 00:01:33.810
this is exactly what they're being exposed to every day of the week.

23
00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:39.810
It's this idea of naturalism, especially those students that are in STEM research type classes,

24
00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:42.810
science, math, et cetera, technology, those kinds of things.

25
00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:47.810
Because the sciences are very much focused on the idea of what we can observe,

26
00:01:47.810 --> 00:01:50.810
what we can see, what we can touch, what we can taste, what we can feel.

27
00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:52.810
Those kinds of things.

28
00:01:53.810 --> 00:01:59.810
But also what they're trying to do is they're trying to not only figure out reality and truth,

29
00:01:59.810 --> 00:02:03.810
but they're trying to figure out meaning.

30
00:02:03.810 --> 00:02:05.810
Meaning naturally, life has meaning.

31
00:02:05.810 --> 00:02:08.810
And this is where it gets a little bit tricky.

32
00:02:08.810 --> 00:02:12.810
Is the naturalism point of view, the naturalism world view, is trying to figure out,

33
00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:17.810
well, what does it mean to have meaning in life?

34
00:02:17.810 --> 00:02:23.810
Well, that's sort of sticky because naturalism actually can't do that.

35
00:02:23.810 --> 00:02:31.810
From the viewpoint of naturalism being told that any meaning in life has to be found then through science.

36
00:02:31.810 --> 00:02:38.810
So science is the only one that can determine what kind of meaning there is.

37
00:02:38.810 --> 00:02:43.810
But science can only take us through the natural world.

38
00:02:43.810 --> 00:02:45.810
It doesn't consider anything else.

39
00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:47.810
And we'll get to more of that in a second.

40
00:02:47.810 --> 00:02:55.810
But what I want you to get though is that this lesson is more than just understanding what naturalism is.

41
00:02:55.810 --> 00:02:59.810
It's being able to see that it can't work.

42
00:02:59.810 --> 00:03:04.810
And that what we want our students to be able to determine is that they're not going to get away from it

43
00:03:04.810 --> 00:03:07.810
because it's the dominant view of Western culture.

44
00:03:07.810 --> 00:03:11.810
But to be able to see things through it and interpret things in line of it

45
00:03:11.810 --> 00:03:15.810
and understand that naturalism might be trying to do these things

46
00:03:15.810 --> 00:03:18.810
and might be trying to explain meaning through science in the natural world,

47
00:03:18.810 --> 00:03:22.810
the natural order of things and all that, but at the end of the day it doesn't really work.

48
00:03:22.810 --> 00:03:24.810
Why?

49
00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:32.810
Well, because naturalism is most closely associated with the idea of atheism,

50
00:03:32.810 --> 00:03:42.210
which means no God, it cannot explain two fundamental questions.

51
00:03:42.210 --> 00:03:52.740
It can't explain the universe in terms of how and it can't explain things in terms of why.

52
00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:55.740
Now, science is trying to do this.

53
00:03:55.740 --> 00:03:58.740
Science is trying to figure out how the universe was created

54
00:03:58.740 --> 00:04:03.740
and how things got here and how it is that we're all here.

55
00:04:03.740 --> 00:04:07.740
But science can't do this.

56
00:04:07.740 --> 00:04:10.740
It can't determine why.

57
00:04:10.740 --> 00:04:13.740
Is it just random? If it's random, why is it random?

58
00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:17.740
We could keep asking that why question over and over and over again.

59
00:04:17.740 --> 00:04:26.740
And to dig a little bit deeper, it also can't do things like determine justice, love, morality.

60
00:04:26.740 --> 00:04:30.740
How do we determine those things?

61
00:04:30.740 --> 00:04:35.740
How do we bring up the reality that there is justice in the world?

62
00:04:35.740 --> 00:04:39.740
How do we bring up the reality that two people love each other?

63
00:04:39.740 --> 00:04:46.740
And that we should have love for our fellow humans and humankind?

64
00:04:46.740 --> 00:04:52.740
Why is killing somebody else wrong even if it might benefit me?

65
00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:54.740
Why is stealing wrong?

66
00:04:54.740 --> 00:05:01.740
Why do we understand some morals to be universal for all cultures of all people of all time?

67
00:05:01.740 --> 00:05:03.740
Where do we get these things from?

68
00:05:03.740 --> 00:05:06.740
See, this is where naturalism falls short

69
00:05:06.740 --> 00:05:09.740
because when we're talking about things like justice, love, and morality,

70
00:05:09.740 --> 00:05:12.740
these aren't tangible things that I can grab hold of.

71
00:05:12.740 --> 00:05:19.740
These are not part of what I can taste, touch, smell, and physically feel.

72
00:05:19.740 --> 00:05:22.740
I can't do that with justice necessarily.

73
00:05:22.740 --> 00:05:24.740
I can't really do that with morality.

74
00:05:24.740 --> 00:05:28.740
These are things that are deeper than what is on the surface

75
00:05:28.740 --> 00:05:31.740
and require a deeper explanation.

76
00:05:31.740 --> 00:05:38.740
So again, it's important for us to understand not only what naturalism actually is,

77
00:05:38.740 --> 00:05:44.740
but it's also important for us to understand that naturalism doesn't actually work.

78
00:05:44.740 --> 00:05:48.740
So in your small groups, especially in your small groups,

79
00:05:48.740 --> 00:05:54.740
make sure you take the time to allow students to sort of discover and discuss

80
00:05:54.740 --> 00:05:58.740
how naturalism sort of manifests itself in Western culture.

81
00:05:58.740 --> 00:05:59.740
What does that look like?

82
00:05:59.740 --> 00:06:02.740
Where do they see that in their schools and in their classes?

83
00:06:02.740 --> 00:06:06.740
It's not just evolutionary science and those kinds of things.

84
00:06:06.740 --> 00:06:07.740
It might be deeper than that.

85
00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:10.740
So get your students to sort of have those discussions discerned.

86
00:06:10.740 --> 00:06:14.740
Get them to see beyond just the surface of what naturalism is

87
00:06:14.740 --> 00:06:19.740
and have them start to analyze it in terms of reality and truth.

88
00:06:19.740 --> 00:06:24.740
One thing great about this Bible study is that we dive into Ecclesiastes

89
00:06:24.740 --> 00:06:26.740
and the things that Solomon wrote.

90
00:06:26.740 --> 00:06:28.740
And I think he had this spot on.

91
00:06:28.740 --> 00:06:32.740
And it just brings so much more to the surface when we consider this book

92
00:06:32.740 --> 00:06:35.740
in light of naturalism.

93
00:06:35.740 --> 00:06:39.740
He writes in the very, very beginning, right out of the gate,

94
00:06:39.740 --> 00:06:43.740
meaningless, meaningless, says the teacher, utterly meaningless.

95
00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:46.740
Everything is meaningless.

96
00:06:46.740 --> 00:06:50.740
What does man gain from all his labor at which he toils under the sun?

97
00:06:50.740 --> 00:06:55.740
Generations come and generations go, but the earth remains forever.

98
00:06:55.740 --> 00:07:00.740
And what he's getting at through this whole book

99
00:07:00.740 --> 00:07:05.740
is the idea that without God putting meaning in life,

100
00:07:05.740 --> 00:07:08.740
life is meaningless, that all of our work and all of our efforts

101
00:07:08.740 --> 00:07:12.740
and everything that we stand for is meaningless.

102
00:07:12.740 --> 00:07:13.740
So think about it.

103
00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:16.740
From a naturalistic perspective,

104
00:07:16.740 --> 00:07:19.740
without God, what is justice?

105
00:07:19.740 --> 00:07:22.740
Without God, what is love?

106
00:07:22.740 --> 00:07:26.740
Without God, what is morality?

107
00:07:26.740 --> 00:07:29.740
Why are we working besides to make money?

108
00:07:29.740 --> 00:07:32.740
Why is there any nonprofit organization at all?

109
00:07:32.740 --> 00:07:34.740
Why do we care about clean water around the world?

110
00:07:34.740 --> 00:07:37.740
Why do we care about any of those things?

111
00:07:37.740 --> 00:07:42.740
Because from a naturalistic worldview, there is no meaning behind those things.

112
00:07:42.740 --> 00:07:45.740
There's no point to doing it because we're all just going to die

113
00:07:45.740 --> 00:07:49.740
and be dust and everything is going to go away and we cease to exist.

114
00:07:49.740 --> 00:07:56.740
But because we have the inclination to want to provide clean water,

115
00:07:56.740 --> 00:07:59.740
because we feel a sense of justice,

116
00:07:59.740 --> 00:08:02.740
because we understand certain morals are universal,

117
00:08:02.740 --> 00:08:06.740
because we love one another and we're called to love one another,

118
00:08:06.740 --> 00:08:09.740
we understand that there's got to be something greater beyond

119
00:08:09.740 --> 00:08:11.740
than just the natural world.

120
00:08:11.740 --> 00:08:14.740
There has to be the supernatural world.

121
00:08:14.740 --> 00:08:17.740
There has to be a Creator God behind all of it.

122
00:08:17.740 --> 00:08:23.740
So we see so clearly, and I want your students to see this as well,

123
00:08:23.740 --> 00:08:28.740
that when we talk about naturalism, it might sound so easy and so simple.

124
00:08:28.740 --> 00:08:31.740
When we talk about that big buffet of worldviews,

125
00:08:31.740 --> 00:08:35.740
it might sound like, oh yeah, this idea works really well.

126
00:08:35.740 --> 00:08:38.740
But when they go down that buffet and they decide that,

127
00:08:38.740 --> 00:08:41.740
yeah, I believe in naturalism and I don't believe in God,

128
00:08:41.740 --> 00:08:44.740
but I believe in taking care of orphans and widows

129
00:08:44.740 --> 00:08:48.740
and providing clean water to children around the world that don't have it,

130
00:08:48.740 --> 00:08:51.740
those two things are now inconsistent.

131
00:08:51.740 --> 00:08:53.740
Those two things no longer make sense together.

132
00:08:53.740 --> 00:08:56.740
It's a great thing that you want to provide that for those people,

133
00:08:56.740 --> 00:08:58.740
but why do you want to do that?

134
00:08:58.740 --> 00:09:00.740
So this goes back to what we talked about last week,

135
00:09:00.740 --> 00:09:03.740
with the idea of it has to be consistent.

136
00:09:03.740 --> 00:09:08.740
So if you're going to desire to want to take care of your fellow man

137
00:09:08.740 --> 00:09:11.740
and take care of people who can't take care of themselves,

138
00:09:11.740 --> 00:09:16.740
if you have that sense of justice and you have that sense of morality,

139
00:09:16.740 --> 00:09:20.220
why?

140
00:09:20.220 --> 00:09:24.220
Again, it takes us back to here and naturalism can't answer that.

141
00:09:24.220 --> 00:09:28.220
So naturally what we would say is that your worldview then needs to adjust.

142
00:09:28.220 --> 00:09:30.220
So help your students navigate this.

143
00:09:30.220 --> 00:09:34.220
This is a little bit tricky and this requires some good discussions.

144
00:09:34.220 --> 00:09:36.220
Again, we'll keep reminding you,

145
00:09:36.220 --> 00:09:39.220
always, always, always let the discussion flow,

146
00:09:39.220 --> 00:09:41.220
let students be honest about what they think,

147
00:09:41.220 --> 00:09:44.220
but challenge them in their thinking.

